Rob Ellwood <rob.ellwood@shaw.ca> wrote in news:3F648930.8AAC011B@shaw.ca:

>      Starvation.  Gehennom is the spirit world, not the real 
> world.  No one has any reason to leave food lying around.  Also, 
> when the spirit of a creature is 'killed', there's no corpse:
> "the body of the red dragon slowly fades away".  

One YANI I had a while back is sort of similar to that. Gehennom is the 
spirit world, and we all know the saying 'you can't take it with you', so 
when you enter or leave Gehennom, everything you are carrying (except 
for a few things like the bell of opening) should either remain where you 
were, or maybe crumble to dust.

Needless to say, this would make the first few levels of Gehennom very much 
more interesting.

Thinking about it further: when you enter or leave Gehennom everything you 
are carrying (even in a container) gets cursed 1 (20%), 2 (75%), or 3 (5%) 
times. If cursed when already cursed then any destroyable object crumbles 
to dust. So you can take an artifact weapon into Gehennom with 95% safety 
provided you bless it first, but if you want to take it back out again 
you'll need to get holy water there. Level teleporting becomes more 
interesting as you'll want to avoid crossing the boundary too often. You're 
going to want an ascension kit plus a set of duplicates before the first 
time you go down, and you'll have to decide whether to risk magicbane, 
mjollnir or whatever. On the plus side, it becomes easier to get unholy 
water or curse objects deliberately.

Of course the curse/dust effect also applies to all that nice stuff carried 
by your pet Archon...

